      * cite17†See all L134: \nL135: Learn more:\nL136: \nL137: cite18†Google DeepMind blog†deepmind.google cite19†Google Research blog†research.google cite20†Google Developers blog†developers.googleblog.com cite21†Google Cloud blog†cloud.google.com L138: \nL139:   * Products & platforms\nL140: ### Products & platforms\nL141: \nL142: See all in Products & platforms\nL143: \nL144:     * Products\nL145: \nL146:       * cite22†Search L147:       * cite23†Maps L148:       * cite24†Chrome L149:       * cite25†Google Health L150:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: EmbeddingGemma 2: an open, lightweight multimodal embedding model\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # EmbeddingGemma 2: an open, lightweight multimodal embedding model\nL215: \nL216: Oct 06, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: EmbeddingGemma 2 is the most capable model for on-device multimodal embeddings, natively mapping combinations of text, images, audio, and video into a unified embedding space.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Sahil Dua\nL225: \nL226: Research Engineer, Google DeepMind\nL227: \nL228: Henrique Schechter Vera\nL229: \nL230: Research Engineer, Google DeepMind\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: cite55†Image: EmbeddingGemma 2†storage.googleapis.com L235: \nL236: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL237: \nL238: Listen to article\nL239: [[duration]] minutes\nL240: \nL241: This content is generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental\nL242: \nL243: [Input]\nL244: \nL245: Voice Speed\nL246: \nL247: Voice\nL248: \nL249: Speed 0.75X 1X 1.5X 2X\nL250: We introduced cite56†EmbeddingGemma†developers.googleblog.com last year to provide a lightweight option for high-quality text embeddings, to help your apps organize, search, and connect information directly on consumer hardware. The developer community’s response blew past our expectations. With more than 20 million downloads, builders have used it to power smarter on-device search tools and privacy-first retrieval augmented generation (RAG) pipelines.\nL251: Today, we’re launching EmbeddingGemma 2, expanding beyond text to unify code, images, video, and audio in a shared embedding space. Built on the Gemma 4 architecture and released under a commercially permissive Apache 2.0 license, EmbeddingGemma 2 has 740 million parameters, making it optimal for on-device inference. It can help find a specific video clip from a voice memo, or search through hours of audio recordings based on a text query, all processed by a single, natively multimodal model.\nL252: Built from the same technology as Gemini Embedding models, EmbeddingGemma 2 is:\nL253:   * Best-in-class for its size: Achieves leading scores among sub-1B multimodal embedders for its size across benchmarks like MTEB (Massive Text Embedding Benchmark) Code and MAEB (Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark), while matching or outperforming many larger models across text, vision, and audio tasks.\nL254:   * Modular by design: Requires as little as 270M parameters for text-only workloads with optional vision (170M) and audio (300M) encoders for full multimodal support.\nL255:   * Storage-efficient: Using Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL), developers can dynamically truncate output vectors from 768 dimensions down to 512, 256, or 128 dimensions. This provides up to 6x storage reduction for local vector databases and memory usage.\nL256:   * Optimized for on-device performance: Runs efficiently within tight resource constraints. With quantization, on a Google Pixel 11 Pro, EmbeddingGemma 2 requires as little as ~191MB active RAM for text-only weights and ~567MB for the full multimodal model.\nL257:   * Extended context ready: Features an 8K token context window (4x larger than EmbeddingGemma 1), allowing it to process up to 5.5 minutes of audio, 29 images, 58 video frames, or interleaved combinations thereof directly on local hardware.\nL258: ## Achieving top-tier quality for code, vision, and audio\nL259: EmbeddingGemma 2 matches the strong multilingual text performance of EmbeddingGemma while delivering a significant 9.92-point improvement on code performance (in MTEB Code, from 68.76 to 78.68), making it well-suited for local codebase indexing, semantic code search, and coding agent retrieval. Across image, video, documents, and audio, it sets a new standard in quality-per-parameter for sub-1B models and even outperforms some specialist models more than twice its size.\nL260: cite57†Image: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (Code)†storage.googleapis.com L261: \nL262: cite58†Image: Massive Image Embedding Benchmark (Lite)†storage.googleapis.com L263: \nL264: cite59†Image: Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark†storage.googleapis.com L265: \nL266: Find full evaluation metrics and model information in the cite60†EmbeddingGemma 2 model card†ai.google.dev .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nORCA: The Annealed Spectral ConditioningOptimizer for Faster, Better LLM Training (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1)\nciteturn41354view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.06116v1\",\"lineno\":209}); Total lines: 498\nL186: Due to computational budget constraints, we focus our comparison on Muon and ORCA. ORCA achieves the lowest final loss at both $25$B and $50$B tokens for the $0.8$B model, showing that its gain persists as the token budget increases. The $8$B experiment extends this result to a larger model trained on $100$B tokens, where ORCA also finishes below Muon (cite71†Figure 4.4 ). Across these MoE experiments, ORCA reduces final validation loss by $0.020$–$0.025$ relative to Muon.\nL187: Early orthogonal shaping also benefits other base optimizers. We apply the same soft orthogonal shaping step (cite53†3.3 ) to Adam and SOAP on LLaMA 130M (cite72†Vyas et al., 2025 ). Shaping is active only during the first $25\\%$ of training; the base optimizer then continues alone. For both optimizers, shaping temporarily raises validation loss while active, but the shaped runs overtake their baselines after its removal and finish lower.\nL188: At 50k steps, it reduces Adam’s loss from $2.8824$ to $2.8435$ and SOAP’s from $2.8640$ to $2.8271$, gains of $0.0389$ and $0.0369$, respectively. These results show that the benefits of early orthogonal shaping extend beyond Muon to other base optimizers.\nL189: (a) Qwen3 160M\nL190: \nL191: (b) Qwen3 0.6B\nL192: \nL193: (c) LLaMA 130M, base optimizers\nL194: \nL195: Figure 4.3: Validation loss across architectures and base optimizers.\nL196: \nL197: cite73†Image: Refer to caption (a) MoE $0.8$B, $25$B tokens\nL198: \nL199: cite74†Image: Refer to caption (b) MoE $0.8$B, $50$B tokens\nL200: \nL201: cite75†Image: Refer to caption (c) MoE $8$B $1$B active, $100$B tokens\nL202: \nL203: Figure 4.4: Fine-grained MoE validation loss with layer-wise release.\nL204: ## 5 Where the Gain Comes From: Controlled Interventions\nL205: cite40†Figure 1.1 offers an initial view of ORCA’s mechanism: early shaping raises the effective rank, part of this increase persists after shaping is released, and loss then decreases faster. While cite10†Section 4 establishes ORCA’s optimization gains, in this section we vary shaping strength and timing, as well as the form of the early spectral intervention, to isolate their contributions to these gains.\nL206: Additional controls for optimizer state and learning-rate schedules are reported separately in Appendix cite23†C.2 .\nL207: Retained Effective Rank Predicts Post-Release Gain We first test whether the effective-rank difference retained after release tracks ORCA’s final gain. Holding all other settings fixed, we sweep the shaping strength over $\\gamma\\in\\{0,0.05,0.1,0.5,1,2\\}$ and measure both the average post-release effective rank and the final validation loss. The sweep exhibits a clear dose response.\nL208: As $\\gamma$ increases, the retained effective rank rises from about $278$ to $335$, while the final validation loss falls from $2.8466$ to $2.8106$ (cite76†Figure 2(b) ). The relation flattens at larger $\\gamma$, indicating diminishing returns. This supports retained effective rank as an important variable behind ORCA’s post-release gain.\nL209: Release-point sensitivity. We first vary the duration of the initial shaping stage over $\\tau\\in\\{0.10,0.25,0.40,0.55\\}$ on LLaMA 130M. Every setting improves over Muon’s final loss of $2.8512$: the four release points reach $2.8210$, $2.8106$, $2.8107$, and $2.8166$, respectively (cite77†Figure 2(c) ). The broad effective range shows that ORCA does not rely on a precise release point. We use $\\tau=0.25$ by default because it is the shortest tested window in the best-performing range.\nL210: Figure 5.1: Loss curves under different shaping windows on LLaMA 130M.\nL211: Shaping-window ablation. We next compare the default early window $[0,0.25T)$ with middle $[0.375T,0.625T)$, late $[0.75T,T)$, full-training, and off-then-on $[0,0.25T)\\cup[0.75T,T)$ schedules. Together with the effective-rank dynamics in cite40†Figure 1.1 , cite78†Figure 5.1 shows two effects qualitatively consistent with the simplified analysis: (i) shaping is costly while active.\nL212: Validation loss rises and remains on a high plateau whenever shaping is imposed, consistent with persistently high effective rank restricting the task-adaptive spectrum. (ii) The benefit emerges after release and requires time. Once shaping is removed, the retained high-rank state supports faster Muon descent without the same endpoint restriction. Early shaping leaves the longest released phase and achieves the lowest final loss.\nL213: Middle shaping also recovers rapidly and finishes slightly below Muon, but remains worse than early; late, full, and off-then-on shaping leave insufficient or no final released phase and fail to recover by the endpoint. These explain ORCA’s two-stage design: raise effective rank early, then reserve most of training for unconstrained optimization.\nL214: ### 5.1 Alternative Early Spectral Interventions\nL215: ORCA’s mechanistic claim extends beyond soft orthogonal regularization: if raising and retaining effective rank early improves optimization after release, other ways of doing so should yield similar gains. We test this with DiagSVD and Pion-start, comparing them with ORCA and standard Muon in 50k-step LLaMA 130M training. Both alternatives replace soft orthogonal shaping during the first $25\\%$ of training; afterward, all variants follow the same Muon dynamics.\nL216: DiagSVD directly amplifies updates along weaker singular directions. It reweights the Muon direction $\\widetilde{\\mathbf{u}}$ in the basis $\\mathbf{U}$ of the left singular vectors of $\\mathbf{W}$:\nL217: \nL218:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathbf{u}}=\\mathbf{U}\\operatorname{diag}(s_{i})\\mathbf{U}^{\\top}\\widetilde{\\mathbf{u}}+s_{r}(\\mathbf{I}-\\mathbf{U}\\mathbf{U}^{\\top})\\widetilde{\\mathbf{u}},\\qquad s_{i}=1+\\theta(i/r)^{\\delta},$$  |  | (5.1)\nL219: where the singular values are in descending order and $\\theta=128$, $\\delta=3$. DiagSVD assigns larger update coefficients to directions associated with smaller singular values, thereby promoting a higher effective rank of the weight matrix. The exact and lazy variants refresh the singular-vector basis every step and every five steps, respectively. Pion-start instead provides a spectrum-preserving intervention. Given the task gradient $\\mathbf{G}$, it constructs the skew-symmetric generators\nL220:  | $$\\mathbf{G}_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G}-(\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}\\mathbf{G})^{\\top},\\qquad\\mathbf{G}_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top}-(\\mathbf{G}\\mathbf{W}^{\\top})^{\\top},$$  |  | (5.2)\nL221: \nL222: and updates the weight from both sides as\nL223: \nL224:  | $$\\mathbf{W}^{+}=E_{2}(\\mathbf{A}_{\\mathrm{out}})\\mathbf{W}E_{2}(\\mathbf{A}_{\\mathrm{in}}),\\qquad E_{2}(\\mathbf{A})=\\mathbf{I}+\\mathbf{A}+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{A}^{2},$$  |  | (5.3)\nL225: cite79†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5.2: Alternative early spectral interventions on LLaMA 130M, released to Muon after $25\\%$ of training.\nL226: where $\\mathbf{A}_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\mathbf{A}_{\\mathrm{out}}$ are obtained from momentum and second-moment estimates of the corresponding generators. The underlying two-sided Lie action preserves the singular spectrum, and hence the effective rank, during the intervention period; $E_{2}$ is its second-order approximation. For Pion-start based methods, whose underlying dynamics preserve the singular spectrum, an orthogonalized start improves the final loss from $2.8505$ with a random start to $2.8371$.\nL227: This offers an indication that early orthogonality matters. The two DiagSVD variants recover part of ORCA’s gain: against Muon’s $2.8512$, DiagSVD exact and lazy reach $2.8265$ and $2.8356$, respectively, while ORCA reaches $2.8106$. By amplifying early updates along directions of smaller singular values, DiagSVD improves the final loss even after release. These all indicate early spectral shaping can benefit later optimization.\nL228: ## 6 Conclusion\nL229: \nL230: ORCA uses temporary early orthogonality regularization to accelerate LLM training, achieving lower validation loss. Its central insight is that optimizers can both choose better update directions and steer early training toward regions where loss is easier to reduce. We hope this perspective inspires future optimizer design.\nL231: ## References\nL232:   * Ainslie et al. [2023] Joshua Ainslie, James Lee-Thorp, Michiel De Jong, Yury Zemlyanskiy, Federico Lebrón, and Sumit Sanghai. Gqa: Training generalized multi-query transformer models from multi-head checkpoints. In Proceedings of the 2023 conference on empirical methods in natural language processing, pages 4895–4901, 2023.\nL233:   * Ambrosio et al. [2005] Luigi Ambrosio, Nicola Gigli, and Giuseppe Savaré. Gradient flows: in metric spaces and in the space of probability measures. Springer, 2005.\nL234:   * Bisk et al. [2020] Yonatan Bisk, Rowan Zellers, Jianfeng Gao, Yejin Choi, et al. Piqa: Reasoning about physical commonsense in natural language. In Proceedings of the AAAI conference on artificial intelligence, volume 34, pages 7432–7439, 2020.\nL235:   * Clark et al. [2018] Peter Clark, Isaac Cowhey, Oren Etzioni, Tushar Khot, Ashish Sabharwal, Carissa Schoenick, and Oyvind Tafjord. Think you have solved question answering? try arc, the ai2 reasoning challenge. arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.05457, 2018.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLoopy: Low-Bit Quantization Framework for Looped Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.05265v1)\nciteturn41354view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.05265v1\",\"lineno\":635}); Total lines: 700\nL615:  | $$\\max_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}_{r}}|\\widehat{L}_{r}(c)-L(c)|\\leq\\varepsilon_{r}.$$  |  | (24)\nL616: \nL617: Let $L_{r,(j)}$ be the $j$-th smallest full-set loss among candidates in $\\mathcal{C}_{r}$, counted with multiplicity. At every stage that eliminates candidates, assume\nL618: \nL619:  | $$L_{r,(K_{r+1}+1)}-L(c^{\\star})>2\\varepsilon_{r}.$$  |  | (25)\nL620: \nL621: Then $c^{\\star}$ survives all screening stages. Exact final evaluation of the survivors on $\\mathcal{D}$ at depth $T$ returns $c^{\\star}$, and the selection regret is zero.\nL622: Proof. Initially, $c^{\\star}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{1}$. Suppose $c^{\\star}\\in\\mathcal{C}_{r}$. If $K_{r+1}=|\\mathcal{C}_{r}|$, no candidate is discarded and the claim is immediate. Otherwise, write $K=K_{r+1}$. For every candidate satisfying $L(c)\\geq L_{r,(K+1)}$, Equations (cite115†24 ) and (cite116†25 ) imply\nL623: \nL624:  | $$\\widehat{L}_{r}(c)\\geq L(c)-\\varepsilon_{r}>L(c^{\\star})+\\varepsilon_{r}\\geq\\widehat{L}_{r}(c^{\\star}).$$  |\nL625: Consequently, any candidate whose estimated loss is no greater than that of $c^{\\star}$ must have full-set loss strictly below $L_{r,(K+1)}$. There are at most $K$ such candidates, including $c^{\\star}$ itself. Thus at most $K-1$ other candidates can precede $c^{\\star}$, even with tied estimated scores, and $c^{\\star}$ is retained. Induction proves survival through every stage. Since the unique global minimizer belongs to $\\mathcal{C}_{R}$, exact final evaluation returns it. $\\square$\nL626: #### Interpretation and relation to the regret bound.\nL627: \nL628: The gap compares the optimum with the first loss outside the retention budget, not necessarily the second-best candidate. For a fixed candidate set, retaining more candidates weakens the separation requirement. More windows may improve estimation but need not monotonically reduce realized error. The strict gap guarantees survival under any fixed tie-breaking rule.\nL629: Unlike the regret bound in Appendix cite41†C.4 , exact recovery here requires uniqueness and separation. The regret bound remains applicable without either condition, including after all optima are discarded. Failure of Equation (cite116†25 ) does not imply elimination of the optimum, since the condition is sufficient, not necessary.\nL630: #### Scope.\nL631: \nL632: The guarantee is conditional on the stated uniform estimation-error bounds and candidate separation, which the fixed schedule does not certify. It does not establish recovery under arbitrary shallow-depth scores, optimality beyond the fixed candidate pool, held-out generalization, or superiority at equal compute. Empirical oracle recovery and selection regret characterize complementary aspects of approximation quality but do not verify these stagewise conditions.\nL633: ## Appendix D H20 Decoding Throughput\nL634: Evaluation protocol. We benchmark Ouro-1.4B at $T=4$ on a NVIDIA H20 GPU, using batch size/input length pairs of 1/128 and 4/2048. Each run generates 128 tokens without EOS stopping, with the first token produced during prefill and the remaining 127 through cached decoding. Aggregate batch throughput is $127B/t_{\\mathrm{decode}}$, measured with CUDA-synchronized wall-clock timing over the complete decoding loop, including Python scheduling but excluding prefill and initialization.\nL635: All methods use fresh BF16 KV caches with 96 recurrent slots, Flash SDPA, and eager execution without CUDA Graphs or profiling. We report the arithmetic mean of ten throughput measurements, collected in two blocks of three warm-up runs followed by five fixed-input measurements.\nL636: Offline and hybrid configurations. Loopy (offline) combines Q/K/V- and gate/up-shared input scaling absorbed into RMSNorm and linear weights, head-wise V–O Hadamard rotation with block size 128 absorbed into paired weights, and up–down scaling that does not cross the SiLU gate. These transforms are shared across recurrent calls and require no explicit online transformation, while activation quantization remains dynamic. This constrained configuration differs from the online Loopy checkpoint.\nL637: The two hybrid configurations add down-input or down- and Q/K/V-input rotations using seed 42 and block size 128 with matching weights. All three configurations reuse compatible input quantization results across Q/K/V and gate/up without fusing their matrix multiplications.\nL638: Kernel differences and diagnostic entries. Loopy uses group-128 Triton packed-W4 kernels, whereas the adapted QuaRot and SpinQuant kernels use per-output-channel weight scales, with additional asymmetric activation zero-point correction for SpinQuant. Attention and timing scope are shared, so the results compare implementations rather than isolate transformation overhead.\nL639: Entries marked $*$ denote W4A4 integer paths whose NLL differences from BF16 evaluation exceed the predefined tolerance; their throughput cannot be paired directly with BF16-path quality results. W4A8 passes this check on four calibration windows, but full quality evaluation was not repeated on the timed execution paths.\nL640: Table 9: Complete H20 decoding throughput (tokens/s, $\\uparrow$). The last two rows add online rotations to Loopy (offline).\nL641:  | W4A4  | W4A8\nL642: Method  | B1/128  | B4/2048  | B1/128  | B4/2048\nL643: QuaRot  | $9.08^{*}$  | $35.14^{*}$  | 8.80  | 34.75\nL644: SpinQuant  | $9.16^{*}$  | $35.85^{*}$  | 9.14  | 35.78\nL645: Loopy (online)  | 10.01  | 40.93  | 10.22  | 41.17\nL646: Loopy (offline)  | 11.21  | 45.45  | 11.37  | 45.83\nL647: + down rotation  | 11.17  | 45.56  | 11.37  | 45.60\nL648: + down/QKV rotation  | 11.15  | 45.26  | 11.25  | 45.66\nL649: ## Appendix E Evidence and Reproduction\nL650: \nL651: The reported results reflect the current experimental pipeline. The main Loopy quantization results use configurations selected by progressive full-depth evaluation, with full-precision results provided as references. Supporting materials include evaluation reports, JSON records for diagnostic figures, and complete-test records for the rebuilt packed checkpoint.\nL652: Figure inputs are stored as paper-local snapshots with source hashes. The accompanying records document the configurations, evaluation protocols, and execution paths associated with the reported results. Hashes identify data and checkpoint versions; numerical agreement and prediction quality are assessed through the corresponding experiments. The supplementary evidence ledger records the scope of these checks.\nL653: ## Appendix F Activation Distributions Across Transformer Blocks\nL654: To examine whether the activation behavior illustrated in Figure cite75†3 (a) extends beyond the displayed block, we visualize the attention and FFN inputs of all 24 physical Transformer blocks in the recurrent core of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. All views use the same 2048-token training window, identity scaling, and block-Hadamard rotation with block size 128 and seed 42. Across all 48 block–input pairs, rotation reduces both the maximum absolute activation and the mean per-token maximum-to-RMS ratio.\nL655: The figures retain all 2048 channels and display maximum absolute values within consecutive 16-token bins; vertical axes are scaled independently, while color ranges are shared within each before–after pair. Blocks are shown in order without omission, extending the block-22 example in the main text; these observations concern the fixed window and recurrent step evaluated here.\nL656: cite117†Image: Refer to caption Figure 8: Activation distributions in blocks 1–4 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL657: \nL658: cite118†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Activation distributions in blocks 5–8 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL659: cite119†Image: Refer to caption Figure 10: Activation distributions in blocks 9–12 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL660: \nL661: cite120†Image: Refer to caption Figure 11: Activation distributions in blocks 13–16 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL662: cite121†Image: Refer to caption Figure 12: Activation distributions in blocks 17–20 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL663: \nL664: cite122†Image: Refer to caption Figure 13: Activation distributions in blocks 21–24 of Ouro-1.4B at recurrent step 4. Each row shows attention input, rotated attention input, FFN input, and rotated FFN input, from left to right.\nL665: ## Appendix G Activation Distributions Across Recurrent Steps\nL666: ### G.1 Activation Profiles\nL667: To complement the comparisons across physical blocks in Appendix cite52†F , we visualize the input activations to the query projection in physical block 12 of Ouro-1.4B across four recurrent calls, using the same input window and the same model state as in the preceding activation analysis. The panels exhibit similar overall structure but differ in their local peaks and maximum magnitudes, illustrating that repeated use of a shared module does not imply identical activation patterns.\nL668: These views provide a qualitative illustration of activation evolution during recurrent execution.\n"
